---
import PublicationLayout from '../../layouts/PublicationLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'publication-status', label: 'Publication status' },
  { id: 'paper-scope', label: 'What the paper studies' },
  { id: 'evidence-limits', label: 'Evidence and limits' },
  { id: 'protocol-relation', label: 'Relation to nomue Protocol' },
];
---

<PublicationLayout
  category="Research"
  type="Publication"
  title="Correctly Rounded or Refused — preprint v0.2"
  description="A preprint on checking the numerical accuracy of paired Student-t test results before software returns them."
  published="August 23, 2026"
  status="Preprint v0.2 — not peer reviewed"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="publication-status">
    <h2>Publication status</h2>
    <div class="notice">
      <p><strong>Preprint v0.2 — not peer reviewed.</strong></p>
      <p>
        Submitted for journal consideration in August 2026. No acceptance decision has
        been made.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      Version 0.2 on Zenodo is the current public manuscript.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="paper-scope">
    <h2>What the paper studies</h2>
    <p>
      Most statistical software returns p-values and confidence limits as numbers, but
      does not say whether the final digits are reliable. This paper tests a stricter rule
      for the paired Student-t test. Software must either return the nearest binary64
      number and prove it is correctly rounded, return a narrow interval containing the
      answer, or refuse to return a result.
    </p>
    <p>
      Binary64 is the standard number format used for most scientific computing. The
      study covers paired and one-sample Student-t tests with integer degrees of freedom
      up to 30. It compares widely used software with an independent high-precision
      reference and tests whether each result can be certified.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="evidence-limits">
    <h2>Evidence and limits</h2>
    <p>
      The manuscript, version history, and persistent identifier are available in the
      <a href="https://zenodo.org/records/22025200" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zenodo record</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The reported results have not completed peer review. They do not show that the
      conclusions of any study using a Student-t test are scientifically valid, or that
      calculations outside the tests and number ranges studied here are correct.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="protocol-relation">
    <h2>Relation to nomue Protocol</h2>
    <p>
      This paper does not define or change nomue Protocol. It may inform future work, but
      its scope differs from the planned Release 1: the paper studies paired and
      one-sample tests, while Release 1 plans to support the Welch two-sample test. Using
      these findings in a future protocol version would require its own specification
      update and verification tests.
    </p>
  </section>
</PublicationLayout>
